    content: `<p>An <strong>inequality</strong> is a mathematical statement in which two expressions are compared using inequality symbols rather than an equals sign.</p>
<p>The inequality sign communicates whether one quantity is <em>less than</em>, <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than or equal to</em>, <em>greater than or equal to</em>, or simply <em>not equal to</em> another.</p>
<p>For any two quantities <em>p</em> and <em>q</em>:</p>
<table border="1" style="width:300px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Symbol</th>
<th scope="col">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>p ≠ q</td>
<td><em>p</em> is not equal to <em>q</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>p &lt; q</td>
<td><em>p</em> is less than <em>q</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>p &gt; q</td>
<td><em>p</em> is greater than <em>q</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>p ≤ q</td>
<td><em>p</em> is less than or equal to <em>q</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>p ≥ q</td>
<td><em>p</em> is greater than or equal to <em>q</em></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Inequalities</strong></h1>
<p>The three principal types of inequalities studied at this level are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Polynomial inequalities</strong> — involve polynomial expressions</li>
<li><strong>Absolute value inequalities</strong> — involve expressions within absolute value signs</li>
<li><strong>Rational inequalities</strong> — involve rational (fractional) expressions with variables</li>
</ul>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Rules of Inequalities</strong></h1>
<p>Understanding the rules governing inequalities is essential for solving them correctly.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 1 — Transitivity:</strong> If <em>p</em> &lt; <em>q</em> and <em>q</em> &lt; <em>d</em>, then <em>p</em> &lt; <em>d</em>. Similarly, if <em>p</em> &gt; <em>q</em> and <em>q</em> &gt; <em>d</em>, then <em>p</em> &gt; <em>d</em>. <em>Example:</em> If Oggy is older than Mia, and Mia is older than Cherry, then Oggy must be older than Cherry.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 2 — Swapping (Reversal of Sides):</strong> Interchanging the two sides of an inequality reverses the inequality symbol.</p>
<ul>
<li>If p &gt; q, then q &lt; p</li>
<li>If p &lt; q, then q &gt; p</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> If Oggy is older than Mia, then equivalently, Mia is younger than Oggy.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 3 — Addition and Subtraction:</strong> Adding or subtracting the same quantity <em>d</em> from both sides of an inequality preserves the inequality direction.</p>
<ul>
<li>If p &lt; q, then p + d &lt; q + d, and p − d &lt; q − d</li>
<li>If p &gt; q, then p + d &gt; q + d, and p − d &gt; q − d</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> If Oggy has less money than Mia, and both receive an additional ₦5,000, Oggy still has less money than Mia.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 4 — Multiplication and Division:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Multiplying or dividing both sides by a <strong>positive</strong> number preserves the inequality direction.</li>
<li>Multiplying or dividing both sides by a <strong>negative</strong> number <strong>reverses</strong> the inequality direction.</li>
</ul>
<p>Formally:</p>
<ul>
<li>If p &lt; q and d &gt; 0, then pd &lt; qd</li>
<li>If p &lt; q and d &lt; 0, then pd &gt; qd</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> If Oggy's score of 5 is less than Mia's score of 9 (p &lt; q), doubling both scores gives 10 &lt; 18 (inequality preserved). However, negating both scores gives −5 &gt; −9 (inequality reversed).</p>
<p><strong>Rule 5 — Negation:</strong> Placing a negative sign in front of both sides reverses the inequality.</p>
<ul>
<li>If p &lt; q, then −p &gt; −q</li>
<li>If p &gt; q, then −p &lt; −q</li>
</ul>
<p>This is equivalent to multiplying both sides by −1.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 6 — Taking Reciprocals:</strong> When both <em>p</em> and <em>q</em> are either both positive or both negative, taking the reciprocal of both sides reverses the inequality.</p>
<ul>
<li>If p &lt; q, then 1/p &gt; 1/q</li>
<li>If p &gt; q, then 1/p &lt; 1/q</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Rule 7 — Squares:</strong> The square of any real number is always non-negative: p² ≥ 0 for all real <em>p</em>. <em>Examples:</em> (4)² = 16, (−4)² = 16, (0)² = 0.</p>
<p><strong>Rule 8 — Square Roots:</strong> Taking the square root of both sides of an inequality preserves the direction, provided both sides are non-negative.</p>
<ul>
<li>If 0 ≤ p ≤ q, then √p ≤ √q</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Example:</em> Since 2 ≤ 7, it follows that √2 ≤ √7.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Solving Inequalities</strong></h1>
<h2><strong>General Procedure</strong></h2>
<p>For all inequality types (especially non-linear and rational), apply the following steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Write the inequality as an equation and solve it.</li>
<li>Identify any <strong>excluded values</strong> (values for which the expression is undefined).</li>
<li>Plot all solution values and excluded values on a number line, using open circles for excluded points.</li>
<li>Identify the resulting intervals on the number line.</li>
<li>Select a test value from each interval and substitute it into the original inequality.</li>
<li>The intervals that satisfy the inequality form the solution set.</li>
</ol>
<h2><strong>Solving Linear Inequalities</strong></h2>
<p>For simple linear inequalities, direct algebraic operations suffice. The critical rule is: <strong>reverse the inequality symbol whenever you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Solve 2x + 3 &gt; 3x + 4</p>
<p>Subtract 3x and 3 from both sides:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>2x − 3x &gt; 4 − 3 −x &gt; 1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Multiply both sides by −1 (inequality reverses):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>x &lt; −1</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong> x &lt; −1</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Graphing Inequalities</strong></h1>
<p>Inequalities in One Variable (Number Line)</p>
<p>When representing the solution of a one-variable inequality on a number line:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use a <strong>closed circle (●)</strong> when the endpoint is <strong>included</strong> (i.e., ≤ or ≥).</li>
<li>Use an <strong>open circle (○)</strong> when the endpoint is <strong>excluded</strong> (i.e., &lt; or &gt;).</li>
<li>Always use an open circle at −∞ or +∞, since infinity is not a reachable value.</li>
<li>Shade to the <strong>right</strong> if the variable is greater than the boundary value.</li>
<li>Shade to the <strong>left</strong> if the variable is less than the boundary value.</li>
</ul>
<p>Writing Solutions in Interval Notation</p>
<table border="1" style="width:350px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Inequality</th>
<th scope="col">Interval Notation</th>
<th scope="col">Circle Type</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>x &gt; a</td>
<td>(a, ∞)</td>
<td>Open at <em>a</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>x ≥ a</td>
<td>[a, ∞)</td>
<td>Closed at <em>a</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>x &lt; b</td>
<td>(−∞, b)</td>
<td>Open at <em>b</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>x ≤ b</td>
<td>(−∞, b]</td>
<td>Closed at <em>b</em></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>a &lt; x ≤ b</td>
<td>(a, b]</td>
<td>Open at <em>a</em>, closed at <em>b</em></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key rules:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Use <strong>square brackets [ ]</strong> when the endpoint is included (≤ or ≥).</li>
<li>Use <strong>round brackets ( )</strong> when the endpoint is excluded (&lt; or &gt;).</li>
<li>Always use round brackets at −∞ and +∞.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Inequalities in Two Variables</strong></p>
<p>To graph a two-variable inequality (e.g., y ≤ x + 4):</p>
<ol>
<li>Rearrange so that <em>y</em> is isolated on the left-hand side.</li>
<li>Plot the boundary line <em>y</em> = x + 4:
	<ul>
<li>Draw a <strong>solid line</strong> for ≤ or ≥ (boundary included).</li>
<li>Draw a <strong>dashed line</strong> for &lt; or &gt; (boundary excluded).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Shade the appropriate region:
	<ul>
<li>Shade <strong>above</strong> the line for y &gt; or y ≥.</li>
<li>Shade <strong>below</strong> the line for y &lt; or y ≤.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><em>Example:</em> For y ≤ x + 4, draw the solid line y = x + 4 and shade the region below it.</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Absolute Value Inequalities</strong></h1>
<h2>Solving Procedure</h2>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Solve |x + 2| &lt; 4</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Treat the inequality as an equation and solve.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>|x + 2| = 4 x + 2 = 4 → x = 2 x + 2 = −4 → x = −6</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Plot x = −6 and x = 2 on a number line; the line is divided into three intervals: (−∞, −6), (−6, 2), and (2, ∞).</p>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Test a value from each interval in the original inequality to determine which intervals satisfy it.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> The interval (−6, 2) satisfies the inequality.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong> −6 &lt; x &lt; 2, or in interval notation: (−6, 2).</p>
<p>Standard Formulas for Absolute Value Inequalities</p>
<p>Let <em>a</em> be a positive real number.</p>
<table border="1" style="width:350px">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Inequality</th>
<th scope="col">Solution</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>|x| &lt; a</td>
<td>−a &lt; x &lt; a</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| ≤ a</td>
<td>−a ≤ x ≤ a</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| &gt; a</td>
<td>x &lt; −a or x &gt; a</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| ≥ a</td>
<td>x ≤ −a or x ≥ a</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| &lt; −a</td>
<td>No solution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| ≤ −a</td>
<td>No solution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| &gt; −a</td>
<td>All real numbers, ℝ</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>|x| ≥ −a</td>
<td>All real numbers, ℝ</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Explanation of Cases 3 &amp; 4:</strong> Since absolute value is always non-negative, it can never be less than a negative number — hence, no solution. Conversely, it is always greater than any negative number — hence, all real numbers satisfy the inequality.</p>
<p>Worked Example</p>
<p><strong>Solve</strong> |2x − 1| − 9 ≥ −5 and express the solution in both inequality and interval form.</p>
<p>Isolate the absolute value expression (add 9 to both sides):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>|2x − 1| ≥ 4</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Apply the formula for |x| ≥ a:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>2x − 1 ≤ −4 <strong>or</strong> 2x − 1 ≥ 4</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Solving each:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>2x ≤ −3 → x ≤ −3/2 2x ≥ 5 → x ≥ 5/2</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Inequality form:</strong> x ≤ −3/2 or x ≥ 5/2</p>
<p><strong>Interval form:</strong> (−∞, −3/2] ∪ [5/2, ∞)</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Rational Inequalities</strong></h1>
<p>Rational inequalities involve expressions of the form P(x)/Q(x), where Q(x) ≠ 0. The critical addition to the standard procedure is identifying and <strong>excluding values that make the denominator zero</strong>.</p>
<p>Procedure</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Solve (x + 2)/(x − 2) &lt; 3</p>
<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Treat as an equation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(x + 2)/(x − 2) = 3</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Solve the equation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>x + 2 = 3(x − 2) → x + 2 = 3x − 6 → 2x = 8 → x = 4</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify excluded values. Setting x − 2 = 0 gives x = 2 (excluded). Plot x = 2 (open circle) and x = 4 (open circle, since the inequality is strict) on the number line. Three intervals result: (−∞, 2), (2, 4), and (4, ∞).</p>
<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Test a value from each interval in the original inequality.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> The intervals (−∞, 2) and (4, ∞) satisfy the inequality.</p>
<p><strong>Solution:</strong> (−∞, 2) ∪ (4, ∞)</p>
<h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Compound Inequalities</strong></h1>
<p>A <strong>compound inequality</strong> combines two inequalities into a single statement.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> Solve −19 &lt; 3x + 2 ≤ 17</p>
<p>Subtract 2 from all three parts:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>−21 &lt; 3x ≤ 15</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Divide all three parts by 3:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>−7 &lt; x ≤ 5</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Solution in interval notation:</strong> (−7, 5]</p>`
