    subtopic: 'Equations From Roots',
    summary_60s: 'When given the roots (solutions) of a quadratic equation, we can derive the equation itself using a straightforward method. This process is often easier than solving for the roots when given the quadratic equation. The general quadratic equation is: \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) where: a',
    key_formulas: '• Standard Form: ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0)\n• Quadratic Formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a)\n• Sum of Roots: α + β = -b/a | Product of Roots: αβ = c/a\n• Discriminant Δ = b² - 4ac: Δ > 0 (2 Real Roots), Δ = 0 (Equal Roots), Δ < 0 (Complex)',
