    summary_60s: 'Circle Theorems are a set of rules or principles that govern the relationships between angles, radii, chords, tangents, and other elements of a circle. Circle Theorems help us understand and solve problems related to circles. Parts of a Circle Centre : The fixed point in the midd',
    key_formulas: '',
    pro_tips_95: 'Official JAMB & WAEC Syllabus Concept: Focus on core definitions, formulas, and past questions.',
    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Circle Theorems in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p><strong>Circle Theorems </strong> are a set of rules or principles that govern the relationships between angles, radii, chords, tangents, and other elements of a circle.</p><p>Circle Theorems help us understand and solve problems related to circles.</p><h2><strong>Parts of a Circle</strong></h2><ul><li><strong>Centre</strong>: The fixed point in the middle of the circle.</li><li><strong>Radius</strong>: A line from the centre to any point on the circumference.</li><li><strong>Diameter</strong>: A line passing through the centre, touching two points on the circumference. It is twice the radius.</li><li><strong>Chord</strong>: A line joining two points on the circumference without passing through the centre.</li><li><strong>Circumference</strong>: The boundary or perimeter of the circle.</li><li><strong>Tangent</strong>: A straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point without cutting through it.</li></ul><h1 style="margin-left:0px; text-align:center"><strong>Circle Theorems</strong></h1><h2><strong>Theorem 1</strong></h2><p><strong>Angle at the Centre Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>The angle subtended by an arc at the center of the circle is <strong> twice</strong> the angle subtended by the same arc at the circumference.</li><li>In other words, if an arc creates an angle <strong> A</strong> at the center and <strong> B</strong> at the circumference on the same side of the arc, then <strong> A = 2B.</strong></li></ul><p>The term 'subtend' basically means to form an angle by drawing lines from the two end points. So, the arc (AC) subtends both angle D and B.</p><p><strong>Theorem 2</strong></p><p><strong>Angle in a Semicircle Theorem (Thales' Theorem)</strong></p><ul><li>Any angle subtended by a <strong> diameter</strong> at the circumference of the circle is always a <strong> right angle (90°).</strong></li><li>If a triangle is formed using the diameter as one side, then the angle opposite the diameter is always 90°.</li></ul><h2><strong>Theorem 3</strong></h2><p><strong>Cyclic Quadrilateral Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>A <strong> cyclic quadrilateral</strong> is a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle (all its vertices lie on the circle).</li><li>The opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are <strong> supplementary</strong> (i.e., they add up to <strong> 180°</strong> ).</li></ul><p><strong>The opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral are </strong><em><strong> supplemetary</strong></em><strong> (add up to 180°). </strong> A cyclic quadrilateral is a quadrilateral that has all of its vertices on the circumference of the circle.</p><h2><strong>Theorem 4</strong></h2><p><strong>Angles in the Same Segment Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>Angles subtended by the same chord or arc in the same segment of a circle are <strong> equal.</strong></li><li>For example, if two or more points on the circumference are connected to form angles from the same chord or arc, these angles are always equal.</li></ul><p><strong>Angles at the circumference in the same segment of a circle and subtended by the same arc/chord are equal. </strong> Both angles are in the major segment and are subtended by the same arc/chord (AE)</p><h2><strong>Theorem 5</strong></h2><p><strong>Tangent-Radius Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>A tangent to a circle is <strong> perpendicular</strong> to the radius drawn to the point of contact.</li><li>This means that if <strong> OT</strong> is the radius and <strong> T</strong> is the point of tangency, then <strong> ∠OTP = 90°.</strong></li></ul><p>The angle between a tangent and a radius in a circle is 90°.</p><h2><strong>Theorem 6</strong></h2><p><strong>Tangents from a Point Theorem</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tangents</strong> drawn from an external point to a circle are <strong> equal</strong> in length.</li><li>If two tangents are drawn from a point <strong> P</strong> outside the circle to touch the circle at points <strong> A</strong> and <strong> B</strong> , then <strong> PA = PB.</strong></li></ul><p>The lengths of two tangents from an external point to the points of contact on the circle are equal. <strong> AB = CB</strong></p><h2><strong>Theorem 7</strong></h2><p><strong>Alternate Segment Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>The angle between a <strong> tangent</strong> and a <strong> chord</strong> drawn from the point of contact is equal to the angle subtended by the chord in the alternate segment.</li><li>If a tangent touches a circle at point <strong> P</strong> and a chord <strong> PQ</strong> is drawn, then the angle between the tangent and the chord (<strong> ∠TPQ</strong> ) is equal to the angle subtended by the chord on the opposite side of the chord (<strong> ∠PQR</strong> ).</li></ul><h2><strong>Theorem 8</strong></h2><p><strong>Chord Bisector Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>The perpendicular drawn from the center of a circle to a chord <strong> bisects</strong> the chord.</li><li>If <strong> OC</strong> is a line from the center <strong> O</strong> perpendicular to a chord <strong> AB</strong> , then <strong> AC = BC.</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Theorem 9</strong></h2><p><strong>Equal Chords and Angles Theorem</strong></p><ul><li>Chords of <strong> equal length</strong> in a circle subtend <strong> equal angles</strong> at the center of the circle.</li><li>If two chords <strong> AB</strong> and <strong> CD</strong> are equal in length, then <strong> ∠AOB = ∠COD.</strong></li></ul><h2><strong>Theorem 10</strong></h2><p><strong>Angle Between Chord and Radius</strong></p><ul><li>If a radius is drawn to one end of a chord and it is perpendicular to the chord, it bisects both the chord and the arc subtended by the chord.</li></ul><table border="1" style="width:500px"><thead><tr><th>Circle Theorem</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Angle at the Centre</strong></td><td>Angle at the center = twice angle at the circumference.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Angles in the Same Segment</strong></td><td>Angles subtended by the same chord are equal.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Angle in a Semicircle</strong></td><td>Angle in a semicircle is 90°.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cyclic Quadrilateral</strong></td><td>Opposite angles are supplementary (add to 180°).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tangent-Radius</strong></td><td>Tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tangents from a Point</strong></td><td>Tangents from an external point are equal in length.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Alternate Segment</strong></td><td>Angle between tangent and chord equals angle in alternate segment.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chord Bisector</strong></td><td>Perpendicular from center bisects the chord.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Equal Chords and Angles</strong></td><td>Equal chords subtend equal angles at the center.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><strong>Example (Cyclic Quadrilateral)</strong></h2><p>From the cyclic quadrilateral TUVW below, find the value of x</p><p>When you have a cyclic quadrilateral, opposite angles is equal to 360⁰</p><p>Sum of opp angles = 180⁰</p><p>80⁰ + (3x + 20⁰) = 180⁰</p><p>3x + 108⁰ = 180⁰</p><p>3x = 180⁰ - 108⁰</p><p>x = 24⁰</p><h2><strong>Example</strong></h2><p>In the diagram below, if &lt;RPS = 50⁰ , ,RPQ = 30⁰ and PQ = QR, find the value &lt; PRS</p><p>Solution</p><p>&lt; PRS = 180 – (50 + 60) = 70⁰</p>`
